WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.800
Larry Karaszewski:&nbsp;&nbsp;Hi, I’m Larry Karaszewski,
nominated tonight for the&nbsp;THE PEOPLE v. O.J.

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.140
SIMPSON, American Crime Story&nbsp;with my partner,&nbsp;Scott
Alexander. Film Courage:&nbsp; Larry, obviously

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.490
this is not your first story about [someone’s
public and private life], you’ve done films

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:14.510
on filmmaker Ed Wood and Larry Flynt, as well.&nbsp;
What attracted you to this story?

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:17.100
Larry Karaszewski:&nbsp; This story, it’s funny,
because it’s the first time [writing partner]

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:22.260
Scott and I’ve&nbsp; have ever written television
and we basically do movies and we would have

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:28.740
never done this story as a movie, because
as a 2-hour package it would have just been

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:34.220
telling people what they already knew about
the O.J. Simpson trial.&nbsp; Whereas all the

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:40.010
offshoots of it, all of the themes we could
discover inside the piece, whether it’s

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:45.160
gender politics or class relations or birth
of the infotainment culture, you know.&nbsp; And

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:51.260
sort of the&nbsp;black problems&nbsp;with the Los
Angeles Police Department.&nbsp; And so there

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:57.970
was so much we could do with this story that
10 hours felt like the proper medium for this

00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:01.300
story. Film Courage:&nbsp; Were you in Los Angeles
during the whole [O.J. Simpson] trial and

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.230
the Bronco [car] chase?
Larry Karaszewski:&nbsp; Absolutely.&nbsp; I remember

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:05.940
exactly where I was when the Bronco chase
happened.&nbsp; I think we all remember where

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:11.900
we were during the Bronco chase.&nbsp; We were
writing a script for Disney at the time.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:15.920
And it was before cellphones and all of those
things.&nbsp; And as everyone is leaving the building,

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:20.370
there was a janitor downstairs with a little
black-and-white television and it was like

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:25.970
“O.J. Simpson is being chased!”&nbsp; And
the entire building all circled around the

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:30.870
janitor’s closet and watched the Bronco
chase [on television] in his closet. Film

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:34.270
Courage:&nbsp; Do you remember the day that the
verdict came out and how the feeling around

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:37.110
the City [of Los Angeles]?
Larry Karaszewski:&nbsp; Absolutely. Film Courage:

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:41.560
So how did this [writing] opportunity come
to you? Larry Karaszewski:&nbsp;&nbsp;One of the producers

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:46.070
on the project, named Brad Simpson, was in
a used bookstore and he saw&nbsp;Jeffrey Toobin’s&nbsp;book

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:51.260
[The Run of His Life – The People v. O.
J. Simpson].&nbsp; And there was this little idea

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:54.810
in Brad’s head like&nbsp;“Maybe I should take
a look at this?&nbsp; Maybe it’s time to revisit

00:01:54.810 --> 00:02:00.520
the O.J. Simpson trial?”&nbsp;And he read the
book and it’s a terrific book.&nbsp; Jeffrey

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:04.170
is also nominated tonight.&nbsp; And the great
thing about this particular award show is

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:07.420
they give the person who wrote the book an
award, as well as the person who adapted it.

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:13.950
It was one of those things where we were looking
for a long time what would be the thing [project]

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:17.580
we would do for television.&nbsp; Scott and I
are usually very deliberate about our decision

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:21.250
about the kind of projects we take.&nbsp; And
in a second we were like “Yes!” in the

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:25.130
room there.&nbsp; We want to take on Tobin’s
book.

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:27.319
Film Courage:&nbsp; So from that time that you

00:02:27.319 --> 00:02:34.560
met with [producer Brad Simpson] how much
time has passed since you got the show on

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.220
the air?
Larry Karaszewski:&nbsp; It was a very long time.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:43.281
We didn’t treat it like traditional television.&nbsp;
We really developed it and researched it and

00:02:43.281 --> 00:02:49.570
so it was about three years until it got on
the air and now about a year since it’s

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:56.990
been on the air. Film Courage:&nbsp; When you
wrote the beginning episode, did you have

00:02:56.990 --> 00:03:01.970
other people look at it [the script] for a
tone and making sure that whatever your own

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:08.140
bias was or your [writing] partner’s bias
was not put in to the writing? Larry Karaszewski:

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:12.970
I would say we definitely had other people
take a look at it, but we have our own tone,

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:18.209
so I don’t actually think it was about us
taking our take away from it.&nbsp; We were never

00:03:18.209 --> 00:03:22.010
coming right out and saying whether he did
or didn’t do it.&nbsp; But there is so much

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:27.600
evidence that sort of point in that direction.&nbsp;
We were just presenting the case in a very

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:35.400
unusual, satiric, comedic tone but the tricky
thing in this was two innocent people died,

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:38.370
so you never wanted that to be any source
of humor.&nbsp; But there was an absurdity that

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:47.440
was the O.J. Simpson trial and having The
Kardashians be a part of it and things like

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:54.250
that.&nbsp; So we were always conscious of trying
to keep our tone and we always had great advice

00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:59.100
and some co-writers with us all the time to
keep us honest.&nbsp; It was very important to

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:02.850
us to tell the truth and do it in an very
entertaining way. Film Courage:&nbsp; I know all

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:06.420
of the legal team became their own celebrity
from it [the trial], Judge Ito included.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:11.200
How much research did you do [beforehand]
on let’s say [prosecuting attorney] Marcia

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:15.030
Clark or whomever? Larry Karaszewski:&nbsp; We
did an insane amount of research.&nbsp; I mean

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:18.620
there is Jeffrey Toobin’s book but every
single person involved in the trial wrote

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:24.430
at least one, maybe two books, sometimes three
books.&nbsp; There is thousands and thousands

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:29.909
of court transcripts.&nbsp; There are hours of
court TV so it was really just the most intense

00:04:29.909 --> 00:04:35.849
research project we ever had.&nbsp; Even the jurors
wrote books.

00:04:35.849 --> 00:04:41.330
Film Courage:&nbsp; So when you write a screenplay
do you think that it’s finished after two

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:44.699
drafts?&nbsp; How critical are you of your own
work or your partner’s work? Larry Karaszewski:

00:04:44.699 --> 00:04:49.129
Well, we’re very critical of our own work
because by the time it gets to even first

00:04:49.129 --> 00:04:55.210
draft, that’s probably actually number ten
for us.&nbsp; We’ve gone through it so many

00:04:55.210 --> 00:05:00.629
times.&nbsp; So we generally prefer the earlier
drafts because they are kind of the most pure,

00:05:00.629 --> 00:05:04.319
but we’ve been in this business a long time
so we do whatever it takes to get it on the

00:05:04.319 --> 00:05:06.759
air.
Film Courage:&nbsp; What book can you recommend

00:05:06.759 --> 00:05:11.340
to some of the viewers on screenwriting structure
or adaption, anything? Larry Karaszewski:

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:18.599
I don’t really know?&nbsp; Certainly in the
old days I would read something like&nbsp;Syd

00:05:18.599 --> 00:05:24.949
Field, but I have no idea if that would actually
be something in today’s climate.&nbsp; I’d

00:05:24.949 --> 00:05:30.409
recommend a book that we wrote which was about
our screenplay for&nbsp;MAN ON THE MOON, the Jim

00:05:30.409 --> 00:05:36.319
Carrey movie about Andy Kaufman.&nbsp; We not
only include the screenplay but we include

00:05:36.319 --> 00:05:44.821
a very in-depth, how things change for the
screen and young screenwriters can really

00:05:44.821 --> 00:05:49.050
look at that book and understand how a script
changes from when you write it until it makes

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:54.139
it on the screen. Film Courage:&nbsp; And lastly,
what film impacted you in terms of a true

00:05:54.139 --> 00:06:00.379
crime movie, like an Errol Morris film say
THE THIN BLUE LINE, different films in a similar

00:06:00.379 --> 00:06:06.029
genre to what you’ve done here? Larry Karaszewski:&nbsp;
I mean as a young man I saw the black and

00:06:06.029 --> 00:06:10.129
white version of&nbsp;IN COLD BLOOD, Richard Brooks’
version of that.&nbsp; And that definitely had

00:06:10.129 --> 00:06:17.939
a gigantic effect on me, it terrified me.&nbsp;
So that was the first thing that came to mind.

00:06:17.939 --> 00:06:23.621
Certainly&nbsp;David Fincher’s ZODIAC&nbsp;is an
extraordinary film, because it’s about not

00:06:23.621 --> 00:06:31.909
solving the crime and it’s about how unsettling
that is.&nbsp; The fact that no matter how horrific

00:06:31.909 --> 00:06:38.210
true crime stories tend to be, at the end
there is some kind of closure and ZODIAC is

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:44.890
about not getting that closure.&nbsp; And what
that does to a person.&nbsp; How it tears you

00:06:44.890 --> 00:06:45.890
apart.&nbsp; So it’s a very interesting film.
Film Courage:&nbsp; Great!&nbsp; Thank you very much.

00:06:45.890 --> 00:06:47.129
I appreciate your time.
Larry Karaszewski:&nbsp; Thank you! Erik Oleson:&nbsp;Hi,

00:06:47.129 --> 00:06:50.580
I’m Erik Oleson. I’m here from&nbsp;THE MAN
IN THE HIGH CASTLE. Film Courage: Erik, how

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:55.060
many years did it take to get this television
show made and how did it finally happen?

00:06:55.060 --> 00:07:03.559
Erik Oleson:&nbsp;I was hired to join the show
for season two. Well, I know from the novel

00:07:03.559 --> 00:07:11.229
which was written decades ago [author Philip
K. Dick], the original producers of the series

00:07:11.229 --> 00:07:18.569
struggled for many, many years to bring it
to the screen. It was a long development process,

00:07:18.569 --> 00:07:23.689
over a decade. So I was fortunate enough to
be brought on in season two. Film Courage:

00:07:23.689 --> 00:07:30.550
Had you already watched the show? Erik Oleson:&nbsp;Oh
yes! I’d watched all of season one. And

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:36.779
I came on board and ended up being the head
writer of season two. Film Courage: Do you

00:07:36.779 --> 00:07:40.259
remember when you sat down to write for them?
I don’t know if you prepared a spec script

00:07:40.259 --> 00:07:45.969
for them or what the process was? What was
the first presentation of your work to the

00:07:45.969 --> 00:07:51.209
show’s developers? Erik Oleson:&nbsp;Well, I
had written on 15 other television shows series

00:07:51.209 --> 00:07:55.259
prior to THE MAN IN THE HIGH CASTLE, so my
work was kind of known. I’m also an executive

00:07:55.259 --> 00:08:00.169
producer so they knew I could handle a lot
of the other aspects that go along with writing

00:08:00.169 --> 00:08:06.229
a big television show like this. But the thing
that I’d written that probably put me most

00:08:06.229 --> 00:08:12.499
in contention, I’d written a pilot about
Nazi occupied Paris, which was a historically

00:08:12.499 --> 00:08:17.080
based pilot. So I think a lot of the folks
read that and said&nbsp;“He knows how to do

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:21.490
a Nazi show.” Film Courage: What are your
first steps for writing a screenplay? Do you

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:28.229
have a set of tried-and-true set of rules
that you use? Or is every script different?

00:08:28.229 --> 00:08:34.650
Erik Oleson:&nbsp;My process evolves every time
I do another job. I realize how much I didn’t

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:43.780
know in my prior job. So I’m someone who
reads pretty much every trick, every screenwriting

00:08:43.780 --> 00:08:49.530
book, every script that I can get my hands
on and I’m constantly trying to evolve as

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:56.100
a craftsman. I am also fortunate enough in
this job to surround myself with really talented

00:08:56.100 --> 00:09:03.120
people and you learn a lot from other writers.
There were writers on THE MAN IN THE HIGH

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:11.740
CASTLE that won Emmys, or Martin Scorsese’s
best friend…you really want to kind of learn

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:16.520
from the people around you and that makes
you better. So I can’t say that I have any

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:26.460
set process, although right now I have to
say I’m a big fan of&nbsp;the John Truby stuff.

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:32.650
I think he kind of hits the sweet spot for
me. But I’ve taken them all. I’ve taken

00:09:32.650 --> 00:09:39.300
[Robert] McKee. I’m one of these guys who
likes to learn new stuff about the craft and

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:45.560
think if I can learn something from any book,
if I can learn one thing from it and it makes

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:51.270
me a better writer, I’m all for it. Film
Courage: Have you ever written a screenplay

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:56.270
that has no structure?
Erik Oleson:&nbsp;I am more of a planner than

00:09:56.270 --> 00:10:04.410
a “pantser.” Obviously like&nbsp;Stephen King&nbsp;will
say he swears by the seat-of-your-pants approach.

00:10:04.410 --> 00:10:12.560
But I’m much more of an outliner, an engineer
when it comes to that. And you throw that

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:19.201
out and you let the character speak to you
and gather the moment and things change. But

00:10:19.201 --> 00:10:25.250
I’m also a big fan of structure I have to
say. So no, I haven’t sat down and tried

00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:35.200
to write a final product from page one. I
kind of construct it over time. I’m a grinder

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:39.360
I guess you could say. Film Courage: When
you begin to write, are you envisioning the

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:44.130
people watching the final product or does
that not even enter into your mind? You’re

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:50.800
so involved with the beginning and what’s
in your own head? Erik Oleson:&nbsp;You want to

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:55.990
keep the audience in mind somewhat but you
also don’t want to pander. Like, if you’re

00:10:55.990 --> 00:11:01.970
in the moment, much as an actor is in the
moment, you’re trying to find the humanity

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:06.950
and the truth behind what you’re writing
about. And whether you’re doing a superhero

00:11:06.950 --> 00:11:11.880
show, whether you’re doing a big, deep,
socio-political show like THE MAN IN THE HIGH

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:18.840
CASTLE you actually find yourself lost in
the moment and how the characters are speaking

00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:26.250
to each other as real people and that’s
the joy of discovery that happens when you

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:33.020
have enough (for me) of the structure figured
out that you have the correct characters in

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:39.520
the scene. You’re in the moment and they
start to talk to you when it works. Film Courage:

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:43.900
Well speaking of talking, this will be my
last question but with dialogue, how much

00:11:43.900 --> 00:11:48.590
are you refining the dialogue and making sure
that you’re researching it for that specific

00:11:48.590 --> 00:11:53.450
time period and trying it out on other people?
Erik Oleson:&nbsp;Constantly. There is a constant

00:11:53.450 --> 00:12:00.010
polish process that goes on for dialogue.
Also, we had researchers who were making sure

00:12:00.010 --> 00:12:09.820
that we didn’t use words that didn’t exist
and sometimes they would catch us making an

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:16.020
anachronistic, modern kind of “Oh yeah.
That doesn’t work. We have to change the

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:21.300
line.” Because we all are creatures of pop
culture, as well. In THE MAN IN THE HIGH CASTLE,

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:27.930
which takes place in the early 1960’s, I
had to avoid references from some of my favorite

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:34.000
superheroes. Film Courage: Any other terminology,
I mean definitely not using the word “Bro.”

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:39.200
That’s become part of the culture. Were
there certain things in the 1960’s that

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:44.400
we don’t even realize it wasn’t part of
the vernacular? Erik Oleson:&nbsp;Correct. In

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:48.860
the case of THE MAN IN THE HIGH CASTLE it’s
an alternative world also where history has

00:12:48.860 --> 00:12:54.680
turned out differently from the tale of World
War II, so a lot of the language that we would

00:12:54.680 --> 00:13:02.100
use in a first draft kind of would be slightly
different like given the way the world turned

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:09.070
out and cultural experience. And in THE MAN
IN THE HIGH CASTLE, white people are not the

00:13:09.070 --> 00:13:15.940
overclass in the Pacific States. They are
kind of treated as an oppressed minority and

00:13:15.940 --> 00:13:22.790
so there are all sorts of textures that seed
into the language when you have that kind

00:13:22.790 --> 00:13:29.250
of story. So it’s an alt-history storyline
and language was very important to us and

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:33.010
we had a lot of very smart people making sure
that we were polishing it up right to the

00:13:33.010 --> 00:13:35.880
moment the cameras rolled.
Film Courage: Excellent! Thank you, Erik.

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:38.860
Erik Oleson:&nbsp;Thank you! Margot Lee Shetterly:&nbsp;
Hello, my name is Margot Lee Shetterly and

00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:44.350
I am the author of HIDDEN FIGURES. Film Courage:&nbsp;
How long did it take to write and then sell

00:13:44.350 --> 00:13:48.400
the book? Margot Lee Shetterly:&nbsp; All told
it took about six years.&nbsp; A little less than

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:54.560
six years, actually.&nbsp; I started working on
it in December of 2010.&nbsp; I really did about

00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:59.870
three years of research before I put together
a book proposal, finding an agent and then

00:13:59.870 --> 00:14:07.779
finding a publisher.&nbsp; So I submitted the
final draft of this book in July of this year.

00:14:07.779 --> 00:14:11.279
And the book was published in September, a
couple months before the movie came out. Film

00:14:11.279 --> 00:14:17.480
Courage:&nbsp; So then how did the movie come
about?&nbsp; How was it discovered and who wanted

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:21.920
to take it on. &nbsp; I know Theodore Melfi [and
Allison Schroeder] did the screenplay (the

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:32.090
adaptation). Margot Lee Shetterly: My literary
agent is Mackenzie Brady Watson and some how

00:14:32.090 --> 00:14:36.980
it landed on the desk of Donna Gigliotti who
is the producer of the movie.&nbsp; So Donna called

00:14:36.980 --> 00:14:42.340
me up and remember this was only a book proposal
this time.&nbsp; It was like three years ago and

00:14:42.340 --> 00:14:47.020
I hadn’t finished the book.&nbsp; She saw the
book proposal and she said “We are going

00:14:47.020 --> 00:14:52.940
to make a movie of this story.”&nbsp; And I
thought “Well, geez.&nbsp; I’m still writing

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:57.740
the book.&nbsp; It’s not even a book.&nbsp; You
want to option my book proposal? I’d never

00:14:57.740 --> 00:15:04.760
written a book before.”&nbsp; But she believe
in that story.&nbsp; She really took a flyer on

00:15:04.760 --> 00:15:11.980
a first time writer/author and now the rest
is history.&nbsp; Everything that she said on

00:15:11.980 --> 00:15:17.481
this first phone call has come true. Film
Courage:&nbsp; I know HIDDEN FIGURES is a true

00:15:17.481 --> 00:15:23.231
story that many did not know of before it
came out.&nbsp; What movie did you see which had

00:15:23.231 --> 00:15:28.250
the same type of impact that I’m sure many
people will feel once they see the film [HIDDEN

00:15:28.250 --> 00:15:32.850
FIGURES]? Margot Lee Shetterly:&nbsp; Wow!&nbsp; That’s
a hard question.&nbsp; There are so many movies

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:38.839
that are amazing and inspirational.&nbsp; You
kind of put me on the spot here. Film Courage:

00:15:38.839 --> 00:15:46.110
[Laughs] Sorry! Margot Lee Shetterly:&nbsp; You
know, I’d have to think about it.&nbsp; In general

00:15:46.110 --> 00:15:51.880
the thing about HIDDEN FIGURES, even though
it is a work of non-fiction, it is totally

00:15:51.880 --> 00:15:56.620
a work of imagination, you know?&nbsp; And I think
the thing about movies that you do remember

00:15:56.620 --> 00:16:02.440
and that do inspire you is that they do spark
your imagination and I think that is the reason

00:16:02.440 --> 00:16:05.990
people are responding so strongly is that
they see something in these women that says

00:16:05.990 --> 00:16:11.680
to them “Wow!&nbsp; I just expanded my imagination
of what the world looks like and who I might

00:16:11.680 --> 00:16:13.320
be.” Film Courage:&nbsp; And you would hear
them [the women working at NASA] speaking

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:17.060
to you, in your own way?&nbsp; In your research
that you did on them….did you hear their

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:21.960
voices? Margot Lee Shetterly:&nbsp; I did!&nbsp; It’s
so funny that you asked that question.&nbsp; I

00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:28.500
found myself talking to them, having conversations
with them and dreaming about them, and thinking

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:33.410
about them.&nbsp; I looked at photos of them to
see what they looked like at different parts

00:16:33.410 --> 00:16:38.960
of their life.&nbsp; I listened to their children
talk about the gestures.&nbsp; Like Dorothy Vaughan.

00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:44.180
When she got upset would start putting her
finger on the table and everybody knew to

00:16:44.180 --> 00:16:51.430
back off, you know?&nbsp; And that she was really,
like a fearless kind of a woman.&nbsp; I knew

00:16:51.430 --> 00:16:58.670
all of these different things about them until
I felt that they had really come back to life

00:16:58.670 --> 00:17:03.310
and that they were my friends.&nbsp; And they
really were.&nbsp; They become my companions.

00:17:03.310 --> 00:17:09.000
Of course in the case of Katherine Johnson,
she is 98-years-old and she is very much still

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:16.010
with us.&nbsp; And I had an incredible privilege
to spend time interviewing her and getting

00:17:16.010 --> 00:17:20.220
to know her life firsthand. Film Courage:&nbsp;
Excellent!&nbsp; Thank you!&nbsp; Best of luck and

00:17:20.220 --> 00:17:22.380
you look beautiful! Margot Lee Shetterly:&nbsp;
Thank you!

00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:27.000
Film Courage:&nbsp; Saroo, from the beginning
of when you wrote your book [A Long Way Home:

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:30.119
A Memoir] until now, how long of a process
was this?

00:17:30.119 --> 00:17:34.749
Saroo Brierley:&nbsp; 2012?&nbsp; April I think?&nbsp;
So this is 2017, so four years?&nbsp; Four and

00:17:34.749 --> 00:17:52.160
a half years.&nbsp; But it took me a year to write
the book.&nbsp; So I was pretty fast. Film Courage:

00:17:52.160 --> 00:17:59.299
Okay.&nbsp; And then how did you present the book
for adaption for [screenwriter] Luke (Davies)

00:17:59.299 --> 00:18:03.629
to write it?&nbsp; Or how did that whole process
turn out. Saroo Brierley:&nbsp; The process was

00:18:03.629 --> 00:18:11.440
Luke read the book and then I said to Cecil
Films and then I said I’ve got to get Luke

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:19.149
to India and take him through the journey
that I went through and I thought that would

00:18:19.149 --> 00:18:28.679
give him another dimension of feel and atmosphere
that you would sort of get from reading a

00:18:28.679 --> 00:18:36.860
book so and I wanted to take him back from
right where it started.&nbsp; Do the train journey

00:18:36.860 --> 00:18:41.149
and all the places that I went to.&nbsp; And then
come to Australia and see if it rings true

00:18:41.149 --> 00:18:45.120
as well.&nbsp; Because I think if I didn’t do
that, it wouldn’t have had clarity.&nbsp; It

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:52.330
wouldn’t be sort of as clean and it would
really sort of help him in terms of writing

00:18:52.330 --> 00:19:00.659
the script.
Film Courage:&nbsp; For me LION has been my favorite

00:19:00.659 --> 00:19:02.879
narrative film for the year and I’m not
just saying this because you’re in front

00:19:02.879 --> 00:19:07.720
of me here.&nbsp; I’m really excited to tell
others about it and I have.&nbsp; The whole package

00:19:07.720 --> 00:19:12.440
is beautiful with the music and everything.&nbsp;
For you, I’m wondering what is an inspiring

00:19:12.440 --> 00:19:16.720
film that has touched your life as LION has
touched mine.&nbsp; It can be from any year.

00:19:16.720 --> 00:19:19.980
It does’t have to be this one [2016-17]?
Saroo Brierley:&nbsp; It’s very relevant actually

00:19:19.980 --> 00:19:21.350
to LION and it’s this other movie.&nbsp; It’s
called the JOY LUCK CLUB.&nbsp; I don’t know

00:19:21.350 --> 00:19:29.399
if you’ve heard of it?&nbsp; Particularly, where
the mother and the children meet in the room

00:19:29.399 --> 00:19:45.480
there…..and at the end of the movie, I meet
my mother and it’s very touching to me.

00:19:45.480 --> 00:19:51.590
I have a very soft spot for that movie.&nbsp;
But it’s an amazing movie that I saw at

00:19:51.590 --> 00:19:56.340
a young age and remember it still to this
day. Film Courage:&nbsp; So when you saw that

00:19:56.340 --> 00:20:01.989
film and you began working with Luke Davies
on the screenplay [for LION], did you want

00:20:01.989 --> 00:20:05.989
people to come away with the same feeling,
or a similar feeling, or really because it

00:20:05.989 --> 00:20:10.559
was about your life you were not able to remove
yourself?&nbsp; Since that movie impacted you,

00:20:10.559 --> 00:20:15.650
did you want others to be impacted, as well?
Film Courage:&nbsp; Well of course I would have.

00:20:15.650 --> 00:20:34.269
That was up to Luke to make that variation
of that certain situation with Garth to make

00:20:34.269 --> 00:20:35.909
it come alive so I guess that’s such a pivotal
point in the movie as well that particular

00:20:35.909 --> 00:20:42.989
bit, reuniting together mother and son.&nbsp;
I think that the way Luke has done it is really,

00:20:42.989 --> 00:20:48.100
really amazing and really wanted to get down
to the grass roots and more particularly that

00:20:48.100 --> 00:20:52.750
sort of scene because apparently that was
the first scene shot and I couldn’t believe

00:20:52.750 --> 00:20:59.070
it when he told me.&nbsp; But a lot of people
were particularly enthralled with this scene

00:20:59.070 --> 00:21:11.750
because it would resonate with a lot of people
especially mothers and people who have lost

00:21:11.750 --> 00:21:21.340
their mothers as well.&nbsp; But I think that
it touches me overtime that I see it.

00:21:21.340 --> 00:21:28.350
Film Courage:&nbsp; What book for you has been
instrumental in the creative process, in writing

00:21:28.350 --> 00:21:34.869
for you.&nbsp; What book [can you recommend] on
writing, creativity? Saroo Brierley:&nbsp; That’s

00:21:34.869 --> 00:21:43.029
a hard one because I’ve only been really
reading magazines and comics and sometimes

00:21:43.029 --> 00:21:51.129
reading Mum’s beauty magazines, Cosmopolitan
and a few others but I’m a first time author

00:21:51.129 --> 00:21:59.460
and I’ve never written a book.&nbsp; And I went
from being into industrial retail sales to

00:21:59.460 --> 00:22:06.440
just landing in this…to just doing this
journey but all the sudden becoming an author

00:22:06.440 --> 00:22:14.060
and having been asked to write my memoir.
Film Courage:&nbsp; And I understand you are working

00:22:14.060 --> 00:22:17.429
with your Mom on a second book.&nbsp; Is that
right? Saroo Brierley:&nbsp; How did you know?

00:22:17.429 --> 00:22:20.669
Film Courage:&nbsp; I heard it in another video…A
little birdie told me. Saroo Brierley:&nbsp; Yeah…we

00:22:20.669 --> 00:22:27.629
are.&nbsp; We’re about to start the prequel
to LION or A LONG WAY HOME.&nbsp; Which doesn’t

00:22:27.629 --> 00:22:36.100
really have to do so much with me but coming
to the point just prior to me which has mostly

00:22:36.100 --> 00:22:55.850
to do with mothers, my adopted mother and
my biological.&nbsp; And also one more lady which

00:22:55.850 --> 00:23:02.539
is Saroj Sood who owns the orphanage and how
those women are all interrelated.&nbsp; And all

00:23:02.539 --> 00:23:04.690
for the one cause, the humanitarian side to
help others that are more in need.&nbsp; And I’m

00:23:04.690 --> 00:23:07.879
just getting goose bumps just talking about
it.&nbsp; It is so unique to have women from oceans

00:23:07.879 --> 00:23:16.039
apart from different backgrounds all wanting
to do one thing and that is to help a child

00:23:16.039 --> 00:23:21.350
and to give him or her another chance of life.
Saroo Brierley:&nbsp; Well I could stay here all

00:23:21.350 --> 00:23:26.980
night and talk with you but I know you have
to go. But last question and that is for people

00:23:26.980 --> 00:23:33.509
of an adult age who have a childhood that
either they don’t remember or there are

00:23:33.509 --> 00:23:37.999
lots of unanswered questions and they’re
adults.&nbsp; What would you recommend to them

00:23:37.999 --> 00:23:42.989
about either going home or trying to find
out more about their own history so that they

00:23:42.989 --> 00:23:46.540
know who they are,&nbsp; [and have] a sense of
themselves? Saroo Brierley:&nbsp; I’m not too

00:23:46.540 --> 00:23:48.499
sure but for me it was finding closure.&nbsp;
And for them I think it was about listening

00:23:48.499 --> 00:23:50.559
to your heart and listening to your dreams
really.&nbsp; Because that is what it was for

00:23:50.559 --> 00:23:51.759
me.&nbsp; In a nutshell, encapsulated.&nbsp; And if
they are longing for something that has been

00:23:51.759 --> 00:23:53.609
lost for such a long time and it’s such
a weight on their shoulders then they ought

00:23:53.609 --> 00:24:01.799
to pursue it.&nbsp; Because life is a little bit
difficult when you don’t know your identity

00:24:01.799 --> 00:24:07.109
and you don’t know your past because…but
give it a valiant attempt and at least try

00:24:07.109 --> 00:24:22.009
to get answers and closure to something that
you can’t get answers to because if you

00:24:22.009 --> 00:24:33.739
don’t try then you don’t know.
Film Courage:&nbsp; Well thank you and good luck

00:24:33.739 --> 00:24:38.359
tonight, Saroo! Constanza Romero:&nbsp; I’m
Constanza Romero and I’m here representing

00:24:38.359 --> 00:24:45.580
my late husband August Wilson.&nbsp; He did the
film adaptation for FENCES and he also wrote

00:24:45.580 --> 00:24:57.059
the original play which played on Broadway
in 1984, 1985 and in 2010 with Denzel Washington.

00:24:57.059 --> 00:25:03.570
Film Courage:&nbsp; Tell us about what you saw
in his (August’s) work ethic and how he

00:25:03.570 --> 00:25:08.400
would dive into a story.&nbsp; How did he create
a world on the page? Constanza Romero:&nbsp; Well

00:25:08.400 --> 00:25:13.359
his work ethic was this:&nbsp; to live was to
work.&nbsp; And to work was to live.&nbsp; He was

00:25:13.359 --> 00:25:21.240
always working.&nbsp; He was always developing
his characters.&nbsp; If we say down to eat he,

00:25:21.240 --> 00:25:27.499
was talking about his work and his characters
were like almost real, you know?&nbsp; He would

00:25:27.499 --> 00:25:35.640
tell me what a character was doing and I was
like “Oh, my God.&nbsp; He robbed a bank?”

00:25:35.640 --> 00:25:46.190
So how he developed it, he wrote in long hand
and then he transferred it to the computer

00:25:46.190 --> 00:25:53.859
and then he would always talk in his rhythm.
Until it sounded good in his ear he wouldn’t

00:25:53.859 --> 00:26:03.720
finalize it yet because he was all about…his
poetry was his music and if it didn’t sound

00:26:03.720 --> 00:26:10.879
right to his ear, he rewrote it and rewrote
it.&nbsp; And that is where you can see where

00:26:10.879 --> 00:26:14.269
he was a poet first.
Film Courage:&nbsp; What can you share with our

00:26:14.269 --> 00:26:20.389
viewers about being so close to someone that
is so involved in the creative process and

00:26:20.389 --> 00:26:26.639
in some sense you are sharing your life with
another entity and that is their work? Constanza

00:26:26.639 --> 00:26:34.619
Romero:&nbsp; Well, I’m a costume designer so
we collaborated on a lot of projects.&nbsp; I

00:26:34.619 --> 00:26:40.950
would be sort of his visual dramaturge so
to speak because I would find different photos

00:26:40.950 --> 00:26:46.010
that were inspirational to him while they
were inspirational to me too because I was

00:26:46.010 --> 00:26:54.539
doing the costumes and he would also say the
lines out loud and I would read his first

00:26:54.539 --> 00:27:00.781
scripts and I would say “Well, what about
this character?&nbsp; Could she be this or could

00:27:00.781 --> 00:27:07.289
she be that?”&nbsp; And so the collaboration
process was really incredible.&nbsp; But sometimes

00:27:07.289 --> 00:27:15.859
it was a little too much.&nbsp; As I said, we
would be downstairs eating and he would go

00:27:15.859 --> 00:27:20.990
on, and on, and on, and on, about the play
and I would just say “Oh!&nbsp; I’ve got to

00:27:20.990 --> 00:27:26.159
go to work.”&nbsp; And he’d follow me up the
stairs and eat a sandwich right by my drawing

00:27:26.159 --> 00:27:33.450
table….but it’s all just good memories.
Film Courage:&nbsp; Any of the characters or one

00:27:33.450 --> 00:27:38.909
specific character that was most like him
in some sense? Constanza Romero:&nbsp; Oh….you

00:27:38.909 --> 00:27:46.049
know…I have to say that all of the characters
are like him and although the character of

00:27:46.049 --> 00:27:56.360
Rose is definitely inspired by his mother.&nbsp;
But I think that this is the amazing breadth

00:27:56.360 --> 00:28:03.629
of his work is that all his characters had
little part of himself but they were also

00:28:03.629 --> 00:28:13.820
so imaginatively created.&nbsp; So I can’t say
that there is one person.&nbsp; For example when

00:28:13.820 --> 00:28:22.679
he was young, he was the older son whose art
nobody understood, you know?&nbsp; Because he

00:28:22.679 --> 00:28:35.690
was writing poetry on napkins and menus.&nbsp;
So the older son Lyons [in FENCES] he was

00:28:35.690 --> 00:28:45.669
sort of like him carrying around his pen and
paper like he carries around his instrument

00:28:45.669 --> 00:28:52.379
and playing at night but nobody in his family
understanding why the heck they don’t get

00:28:52.379 --> 00:28:59.330
a job.&nbsp; Right! Yes! Film Courage:&nbsp; And lastly,
what can you tell others about what it’s

00:28:59.330 --> 00:29:03.299
like to share your life with someone who at
some points maybe they are critical of their

00:29:03.299 --> 00:29:07.669
own work?&nbsp; And watching them go through days
that maybe they didn’t feel good about their

00:29:07.669 --> 00:29:12.429
projects even though others related to them
but it was such a tough process because they

00:29:12.429 --> 00:29:17.159
were so close to the material and couldn’t
see it for what it was? Constanza Romero:

00:29:17.159 --> 00:29:21.909
Yeah…August hated to cut.&nbsp; If somebody
said the play is longer than three hours,

00:29:21.909 --> 00:29:30.369
what are we going to do?&nbsp; So I think that
he would be so stubborn, nobody could make

00:29:30.369 --> 00:29:35.960
him cut something.&nbsp; And then finally one
of the producers set me aside and said “Can

00:29:35.960 --> 00:29:42.179
you tell him that Seven Guitars is a little
too long.”&nbsp; And actually I sat down and

00:29:42.179 --> 00:29:47.230
I could really see where it could be snipped
here and there and I was able to help him

00:29:47.230 --> 00:29:53.360
do that.&nbsp; But I believe I was one of the
only people he really trusted to tell him

00:29:53.360 --> 00:30:06.090
the truth as you’re saying.&nbsp; And not every
day was inspired, not every day was a joy.

00:30:06.090 --> 00:30:13.590
Creating something is not always a joyful
process. &nbsp; It has a lot of raw emotions and

00:30:13.590 --> 00:30:22.769
you are practically weaving gold.&nbsp; But the
raw material is not always the gold that it

00:30:22.769 --> 00:30:26.149
becomes later.
Film Courage:&nbsp; Beautiful!&nbsp; Thank you! Howard

00:30:26.149 --> 00:30:31.070
A. Rodman:&nbsp; Hi,&nbsp; I’m Howard Rodman.&nbsp;
I’m president of the Writer’s Guild West

00:30:31.070 --> 00:30:36.410
and also Chair of the 2017 USC Scripter Selection
Committee. Film Courage: &nbsp; We’re curious

00:30:36.410 --> 00:30:41.369
what you would recommend to beginning writers
on trying to present their first screenplay

00:30:41.369 --> 00:30:44.879
or protect their work.&nbsp; What are some of
the few things you recommend? Howard A. Rodman:

00:30:44.879 --> 00:30:49.590
First thing I recommend to beginning writers
is that the only way that I absolutely guarantee

00:30:49.590 --> 00:30:56.409
that you will not have a career as a writer
is to quit.&nbsp; So what I recommend to beginning

00:30:56.409 --> 00:31:02.540
writers is, real simple, real basic, is that
you write every day in terms of getting our

00:31:02.540 --> 00:31:08.009
work out there in the world.&nbsp; If you’re
shy or a shoegazer, you have to be aggressive

00:31:08.009 --> 00:31:12.090
on behalf of your work and get it into the
hands of anyone who might conceivably read

00:31:12.090 --> 00:31:15.729
it, as painful and humiliating as that process
is.

00:31:15.729 --> 00:31:19.800
In terms of protecting your work you should
absolutely copyright it.&nbsp; Put the C in the

00:31:19.800 --> 00:31:23.610
circle.&nbsp; Get the form from the U.S. Copyright
Office.&nbsp; The forms are online.&nbsp; It’s really

00:31:23.610 --> 00:31:30.179
simple.&nbsp; And also register with the Writer’s
Guild.&nbsp; The two give very complimentary protections

00:31:30.179 --> 00:31:36.389
for work for new people.
Film Courage:&nbsp; You are a writer, as well?

00:31:36.389 --> 00:31:41.119
You’ve written screenplays?&nbsp; Can you share
with us the first film that inspired you to

00:31:41.119 --> 00:31:43.760
want to write?
Howard A. Rodman:&nbsp; I’m a writer.&nbsp; See

00:31:43.760 --> 00:31:48.610
the calluses [holds up his hands to the camera].&nbsp;
And there are so many films that inspired

00:31:48.610 --> 00:31:56.940
me to want to write that to pick one I think
would be crazy.&nbsp; But I think I remember there

00:31:56.940 --> 00:32:05.320
is a scene at the opening of an old Film Noir
boxing movie called BODY AND SOUL, I believe

00:32:05.320 --> 00:32:10.109
written by Abraham Lincoln Polonsky, Directed
by Robert Rossen.&nbsp; And there is a boxer,

00:32:10.109 --> 00:32:16.879
a very proud, sort of first generation, scrappy
immigrant boxer played by John Garfield and

00:32:16.879 --> 00:32:21.580
the mob boss is offering him a lot of money
not to win the fight.&nbsp; All the money has

00:32:21.580 --> 00:32:26.619
been bet the other way.&nbsp; And John Garfield
starts talking about his pride and his morals

00:32:26.619 --> 00:32:32.049
and the ethics of throwing a fight and the
mob boss turns to him and says “There is

00:32:32.049 --> 00:32:37.730
addition.&nbsp; There is subtraction.&nbsp; The rest
is conversation.”&nbsp; And I just thought “Wow!

00:32:37.730 --> 00:32:43.619
If I could just like write one of those!”
And I think for many of us we start reading

00:32:43.619 --> 00:32:49.009
books and watching movies because somebody
has built a world and we can just hide in

00:32:49.009 --> 00:32:53.629
that world for awhile and then you realize.&nbsp;
“Oh, wait!&nbsp; I could build me own sort of

00:32:53.629 --> 00:32:58.309
purpose-built world that would really fit
me.”&nbsp; And so the transition from movie

00:32:58.309 --> 00:33:04.750
going to reading to writing I think for many
of us is just a slow descent into the pit.

00:33:04.750 --> 00:33:09.980
Film Courage:&nbsp; So the first screenplay that
you wrote did you have the similar sort of

00:33:09.980 --> 00:33:13.630
dream that this was going to be the first
ONE and it would put you on the map?&nbsp; Like

00:33:13.630 --> 00:33:18.123
so many new writers think that the first screenplay
will open doors.&nbsp; Actually even the first

00:33:18.123 --> 00:33:23.119
draft [of a first screenplay]. Howard A. Rodman:
&nbsp; I never had any thoughts like that.&nbsp; I

00:33:23.119 --> 00:33:29.399
spent well over a decade writing unpublishable
novels before I transitioned to writing unproduceable

00:33:29.399 --> 00:33:34.240
screenplays.&nbsp; So I was under no illusion.&nbsp;
I just started having ideas that were movies

00:33:34.240 --> 00:33:40.289
that weren’t quite books.&nbsp; And the first
of those screenplays that I wrote still has

00:33:40.289 --> 00:33:46.379
not been produced but I’ve gotten tons and
tons of work off of it.&nbsp; It’s like of course

00:33:46.379 --> 00:33:52.470
“We can’t do this because it’s too weird,
BUT maybe you can do that?”&nbsp; And so I don’t

00:33:52.470 --> 00:33:57.629
think I ever really expected frankly that
anything I wrote would ever be filmed.&nbsp; And

00:33:57.629 --> 00:34:02.279
when things I write do get filmed, it just
strikes me as sort of the bonus rounds in

00:34:02.279 --> 00:34:07.129
a pinball game.&nbsp; It’s sort of unexpected
and really lovely. Film Courage: Wrapping

00:34:07.129 --> 00:34:12.090
up here.&nbsp; I know you have to go. &nbsp; Wondering
what you see as the most common mistakes or

00:34:12.090 --> 00:34:17.140
somethings new writers should be weary of
regarding formatting, structure, things like

00:34:17.140 --> 00:34:19.670
that?
Howard A. Rodman:&nbsp; I would tell beginning

00:34:19.670 --> 00:34:24.419
writers not to worry too much about about
formatting and structure.&nbsp; In terms of formatting

00:34:24.419 --> 00:34:31.630
there are many really good software programs
and some of them you can get for free and

00:34:31.630 --> 00:34:37.020
so let them worry about the formatting.&nbsp;
In terms of the structure, I think you can’t

00:34:37.020 --> 00:34:43.260
say “My God!&nbsp; My first act didn’t end
on page 29.&nbsp; I’m a bad writer” if you

00:34:43.260 --> 00:34:49.900
find real living characters through telling
a story that only you can tell or write about

00:34:49.900 --> 00:34:54.780
a place in the world that you know better
than anybody else, just let the story tell

00:34:54.780 --> 00:34:59.540
itself, or let the characters tell the story
and the rest will fall into place. Film Courage:

00:34:59.540 --> 00:35:04.550
So in other words, for lack of a better term,
get the vomit draft, please excuse my crass

00:35:04.550 --> 00:35:09.530
analogy, but get that out and worry about
all the other things later? Howard A. Rodman:

00:35:09.530 --> 00:35:14.970
No, I wouldn’t even say that.&nbsp; I would
say…I was the editor-in-chief of my college

00:35:14.970 --> 00:35:19.590
newspaper.&nbsp; And there is a big sign in the
newspaper office underneath this huge clock,

00:35:19.590 --> 00:35:24.650
that had a blood red second hand and the sign
said “This is a daily, not a weekly.”

00:35:24.650 --> 00:35:29.320
And whenever we were there past midnight the
managing editor would put up another sign

00:35:29.320 --> 00:35:33.130
that would say “Don’t get it right, get
it written.”&nbsp; And I would say the same

00:35:33.130 --> 00:35:36.770
thing to anyone starting out writing and to
people who have been writing all of their

00:35:36.770 --> 00:35:38.369
lives. Film Courage:&nbsp; I love it!&nbsp; Thank
you!

